# Proposed exact design-principles delta

This is a proposed section delta for the integrating parent, not a replacement of another worker's document.

## Insert after “Understand from the task and visible things”

### Plain language, open questions and explanatory wonders

Latest clarification: 29 October 2026.

- Use plain, clear English in every story popup, item explanation and wonder. The puzzle is combining things, not decoding mysterious prose. Explain what a card means directly; do not require the player to read its input cards or remember earlier popups to understand a new result.
- Keep authoring and research commentary out of player prose: no discussion of invented meetings, editorial comparisons, schema mechanics or audit defenses. Preserve the actual disagreement and meaningful limits of the evidence instead. A layer's order does not date it; one writer's belief is not every tradition's belief. Research qualifications and citations remain in private metadata and research records.
- Ideas and Divinity objectives should be short, concrete questions that open an inquiry, not commands that announce the required conclusion. “What is the nature of evil?” and “Whose fault is fire?” are examples. A completed task means the player has explored or reconstructed the relevant position, not settled the philosophical question for everyone. Do not change objective requirements or conceal needed visible context merely to make a question sound open-ended.
- Ideas is an explicit trial of frequent explanatory wonders for significant created concepts, observations, methods and enabling technologies. Review the actual outputs and record coverage with a denominator and specific exclusions; do not manufacture extra recipes or celebrate every mundane preparation to reach a quota. Ordinary food packing and repeated applications can remain uncelebrated. Divinity may use the same approach where an explanation helps.
- An explanatory wonder starts with a self-contained paragraph, followed by the current resulting item's name in italics. Do not add a separate rhetorical title, author/source chrome or recipe checklist. The paragraph must restate enough of the problem and the relevant claims to stand alone. Accurate quotations can remain, but should have an ordinary-language explanation; never silently change wording while presenting it as an exact quotation.
- Primitives can have ordinary double-tap explanations through `Item.reading.text` or `description`. They must not receive fake creation milestones. Existing creation and functional milestone predicates remain meaningful; explanatory replay must not create, acknowledge or alter progress merely because an explanation was read.
- Do not display chapter titles. A meaningful real place or era can appear as an italic context line at the start of a location popup. Do not disguise a topic title as a place name or repeat the same era heading at each small session.
- An evening or next-day session at the same study table can explain a cleared workspace more honestly than inventing an alcove for every topic. Keep earlier panels and their work tangible, bring only the actual items needed for the new session, and preserve the historical dates of the writings. A fictional study schedule is not a claimed documented meeting between authors.
- Attribution can be contextual on item labels. Within Augustine's home study, omit a leading “Augustine:” while retaining “Case:” and “Paul:”. Keep the full underlying label and restore attribution when the item is physically carried into a mixed-thinker setting. Resolve labels by the item's actual owning location, including old panels and fitted components; reserve enough space for the restored label.

## Reconcile the older “Rare milestones celebrate new possibilities” section

Replace its first bullet with:

- Physical invention celebrations remain selective and reward genuinely new possibilities. Ideas and Divinity can additionally use the frequent, explanatory wonder policy above; those popups explain concepts rather than making every recipe a triumph. Chapter narration, explanatory wonders and physical celebrations have different jobs.

Replace its evocative-language bullet with:

- Use concise, ordinary language that explains the achieved possibility. Avoid riddles, research commentary, recipe sequences, unlimited-resource promises and claims of capabilities the game has not earned.

## Reconcile the older reading and story-card bullets under “Play without administration”

Replace the ordinary-story-card bullet with:

- Ordinary story cards contain clear prose without chapter titles, duplicate speakers, source-attribution chrome or goal checklists. A meaningful real era/place may appear as a single italic context line. Essential distinctions remain in the prose; source citations remain private.

Replace the readable-manuscript bullet with:

- Ordinary things, including manuscripts and primitives, open their explanation on intentional double-tap. Prefer authored `Item.reading.text`, then `description`; do not show source citations or attribution chrome. Historical names needed to distinguish actual claims may remain in the explanation. Reading preserves selection and scroll, changes no progress, and pauses a pending handoff until dismissal.
